 ## Overview
-Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and its variants synthesize novel views of 3D scenes from a sparse set of posed 2D images by learning a continuous function that maps spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, S5][S6, S7, unofficial]. What sets the family apart from explicit grid-based siblings like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization](4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md) is the use of neural networks (typically MLPs) to represent the scene implicitly, allowing for high-fidelity reconstruction of complex geometry and view-dependent appearance without explicit mesh or voxel structures [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, S5][S6, S7, unofficial].
+Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) represent a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric function that maps 3D spatial coordinates and 2D viewing directions to emitted RGB color and volume density [S1][S5][S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of novel view synthesis by replacing explicit geometric primitives (meshes or point clouds) with an implicit neural representation encoded in network weights, enabling photorealistic rendering of complex scenes with view-dependent effects like specularities and transparency [S1][S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its parent method [Radiance-field optimization](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) by introducing a specific asymmetric MLP architecture that injects viewing direction near the output layer, and a two-stage hierarchical sampling strategy (coarse and fine networks) to efficiently allocate samples to opaque regions [S2, unofficial][S4][S7, unofficial].
 
